---
// Reporting a sprinkler problem. The whole point of the map is that a
// resident can write "Zone 7" instead of "the one by the mailboxes", so
// this page's job is to turn a sighting into a report a contractor can
// act on without a second visit to ask where it was.
//
// The four categories below are hardcoded on purpose. They are the same
// vocabulary as IssueCategory in types.ts and they change only when that
// type changes, so there is no YAML file behind them.
//
// Routing: reports go to the property manager's support ticket form,
// reached from the property manager's own public page. No individual
// contact route is published here.
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { getAssociation, getNotices } from "../../lib/data";
import { withBase } from "../../lib/kit/base";

const association = getAssociation();
const sourceUrl = association?.sourceUrl;
const sprinklerNotice = getNotices().find((n) => n.id === "notice-sprinklers");

const categories = [
  {
    name: "Dead spots",
    covers: "Grass or plants drying out while everything around them stays green.",
    examples: "A brown patch that keeps growing, a strip along a sidewalk that never recovers, a shrub bed that looks dry after a watering night.",
  },
  {
    name: "Schedule and timing",
    covers: "Water running at the wrong time, for too long, or not at all.",
    examples: "Sprinklers on in the middle of a hot afternoon, running during rain, a zone that never seems to come on.",
  },
  {
    name: "Equipment failure",
    covers: "A broken or misaimed part anywhere in the system.",
    examples: "A snapped head, a head buried below the grass line, a head watering the sidewalk or a fence instead of the lawn, a leaking valve box.",
  },
  {
    name: "Water waste",
    covers: "Water leaving the property instead of soaking in.",
    examples: "Water fountaining from a broken head, runoff down the gutter, a soggy area that stays wet between watering nights.",
  },
];
---
<Base title="Report a landscaping problem">
  <nav class="subnav" aria-label="Landscaping sections">
    <a href={withBase("/landscaping")}>Map</a>
    <a href={withBase("/landscaping/watering")}>Watering times</a>
    <a href={withBase("/landscaping/trees")}>Trees</a>
    <a href={withBase("/landscaping/issues")}>Issues</a>
    <a href={withBase("/landscaping/water")}>Water use</a>
    <a href={withBase("/landscaping/report")} aria-current="page">Report a problem</a>
  </nav>

  <div class="eyebrow">Landscaping</div>
  <h2>Report a problem</h2>
  <p>
    A report gets fixed faster when it says where the problem is in terms the
    crew can find. "The sprinkler by the mailboxes" means nothing to someone
    who has never stood at your mailbox. "Zone 7" sends them to the right
    valve on the first trip.
  </p>

  <p class="notice">
    <strong>Water fountaining from a broken head is the urgent case.</strong>{" "}
    A snapped head pushes far more water in one cycle than the zone would use
    watering normally, and the association pays for every gallon of it. Report
    it the same day you see it rather than holding it until you have something
    else to report alongside it.
  </p>

  <h3>What to include</h3>
  <p>
    Four things. Miss one and someone has to come back out to collect it.
  </p>
  <ul class="agenda">
    <li>
      <strong>The zone number.</strong> Find it on the{" "}
      <a href={withBase("/landscaping")}>irrigation map</a>. If the shape you
      need is not on the map yet, name the nearest fixed feature you can
      point to on the map instead, and say so.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A photo.</strong> One wide shot showing where it is, and one
      close shot of the head or the patch itself. A photo settles most
      questions a work order would otherwise generate.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The time of day it was running.</strong> Watering runs on a
      timed schedule, so the clock time is what identifies which program and
      which zone was active. "Around 6:15 in the morning" is enough.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>What it was doing.</strong> Fountaining, not turning on,
      spraying the sidewalk, or a brown patch that is not recovering. Plain
      description beats a diagnosis.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>The four categories the association tracks</h3>
  <p>
    Every confirmed problem gets filed under one of these. Using the same
    words in your report keeps it from being logged twice or filed under the
    wrong heading.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Category</th>
          <th>What it covers</th>
          <th>Examples</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {categories.map((c) => (
          <tr>
            <td><strong>{c.name}</strong></td>
            <td>{c.covers}</td>
            <td>{c.examples}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h3>Where to send it</h3>
  <div class="card">
    <p style="margin-top:0">
      Reports go to the property manager through their support ticket form.
      The form is linked from the property manager's Canyon Breeze page,
      along with their office hours and phone number. Submitting a ticket
      rather than sending a message to someone directly is what puts the
      report in a queue that can be tracked and followed up on.
    </p>
    {sourceUrl ? (
      <p style="margin-bottom:0">
        <a href={sourceUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
          Open the property manager's Canyon Breeze page
        </a>
      </p>
    ) : (
      <p style="margin-bottom:0">
        The property manager's page is not linked here yet. Ask a board
        member for the current support ticket address.
      </p>
    )}
  </div>

  {sprinklerNotice && (
    <>
      <h3>The association's own notice</h3>
      <p>
        Sent to all homeowners. It asks for the same two things this page
        does: report the fountaining heads quickly, and send photos.
      </p>
      <div class="card">
        <blockquote class="report-quote">
          <p style="margin-top:0">{sprinklerNotice.text}</p>
          <footer class="report-quote__source">
            Homeowner notice: {sprinklerNotice.title}
          </footer>
        </blockquote>
      </div>
    </>
  )}

  <h3>What happens next</h3>
  <p>
    A report goes to the property manager,
    who decides whether it needs a contractor visit, a schedule change, or
    nothing at all. There is no automatic ticket status on this site, and no
    promised response time.
  </p>
  <p>
    Once a problem is confirmed, it gets logged against its zone and shows up
    on the <a href={withBase("/landscaping/issues")}>issues page</a> with its
    category, its severity, and the date it was opened. That page is the only
    place this site tracks progress, and it only lists confirmed problems, not
    every report received.
  </p>
</Base>

<style>
  /* The quoted notice needs to read as somebody else's words rather than
     site copy. Border and muted source line only, no new colours. */
  .report-quote {
    margin: 0;
    padding-left: 1rem;
    border-left: 3px solid var(--accent-border);
  }
  .report-quote__source {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }
</style>
